Z||000007||fierogt88||04-12-2001||02:09 PM||||Maybe I should just call archie, something tells me he has done it at least once.  For the rest of you, I'd still like to know a couple things... <P>4.3 out of later model blazer/s10 (anything else have them? ) going into an 88 gt automatic.<P>A) Does anybody know about the electronics on these things and what they expect to see?  Does it need feedback from ABS / transmission / etc. to work?<P>B) Places to look for performance parts for them.  Does anybody know if the 4.3 is used in any kind of racing series?  Boats maybe?  Sprint cars maybe?<P>I'm going to call Archie to find out about the mounting and stuff.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||rforbes||04-12-2001||02:39 PM||||Mercruiser does use a GM 4.3 in a marine application.  I don't know all the details but I'm sure there would be a lot of performance mods for this engine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||04-12-2001||03:23 PM||||My 1994 GMC Safari has the enhanced 4.3 engine which is listed in the GMC sales brochure as 200 hp.  It is very strong for a v-6 and moves the heavy van nicely. Coupled with the limited slip diff. it does a great job towing my ski boat up slippery ramps.<P>There is a 4.3 powered Fiero in the Tuscaloosa, Alabama area for sale for $1950.  I spoke to the guy selling it.  He apparently took it in trade from a guy that built it for autocross.  It is a built engine with roller cam etc. (carb. not FI) and is supposed to be very fast.  The A/C compressor has not been mounted otherwise it is a loaded car.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierojunky||04-12-2001||04:02 PM||||the Silverado also uses a 4.3 Standard Vortec 4300 V6 SFI engine with 200 horsepower @ 4600 rpm and 260 lb.-ft. of torque @ 2800 rpm  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||owenslee||04-12-2001||06:22 PM||||Have considered this conversion myself...but not with the computer controlled engine.<P>I have access to a 4.3 Bow Tie racing block with 4.125 bore and a common journal forged crank with 3.75" stroke.<P>Using V-8 rods and 400 V-8 pistons this engine will provide 302 cu. in.<P>I also have a set of aluminum heads 2.02 x 1.6 heads, racing / baffled oil pan and a "rat roaster" intake.<P>This combination will conservatively produce 340 HP and remain streetable.<P>The only reason I have not made the swap is current Missouri Emmission regulations.<P>I would be a nice ride...<P>BTW the 4.3 is exactly 4.40" shorter than a chevy small block. <P>Most of the v-8 kit components should be useable, some as is, some with minor relocation or alteration.<P>Less weight than V-8 and no need to cut the Fiero...<P>Go for it!<P>Kerry<P>------------------<BR>"Badges?...Badges..??? We don gat to sho yu no stinkin' BADGES..!!!"||||reg||6
Z||000012||Nashco||04-12-2001||06:37 PM||||Well, I'm working on putting a 4.3 into my VW baja bug, so I have a little insight here.  I don't know much about the location of the v6 and v8 engines in comparison, but the tranny bolt up is the exact same.  As far as pistons, connecting rods, water pumps, and accessories, they're all the same too.  There's a lot of aftermarket cams, cranks, and intakes available; it's just a matter of what you're looking to get/spend.  The 4.3 was used in (I think busch) stock car racing for a few years, so there's anything from stock parts to race car parts available.  I'm planning on using an '89 full size truck motor, which I got the complete wiring harness for.  I'm going to use the entire GM engine management setup (wiring harness, sensors, ECM) and bolt it up to a porsche tranny.  The engine should be self reliant, without outside factors like ABS or transmission affecting it.  Remember, this is for an '89...I'm not sure what the latest years are like, I'm sure they use a VSS.<P>My best guess as to the 96-98 difference between other years is that the 96 and later motors have a counter-balance shaft for a smoother engine, and I think after 95 all 4.3s are vortec, rather than throttle body.  The vortec motor produces more power, but I chose the t.b. for reliability.  The vortec motors require an exact amount of fuel pressure, with about a 7 lb. variance allowed.  Anything less and they will not run, at all (since this is going in an offroad car, I need to at least be able to chug away).<P>I chose the 4.3 for the same reason as you, it's original for a swap, but still really cheap and easy to get parts for compared to others.  I don't know of any specific retailers to tell you about, it really depends on what you're looking for.<P>Well, I hope this answered some of your questions.  If you have more, post them on here because I'd like to see what people say too <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Happy Trails<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Jay||04-12-2001||10:02 PM||||There is a guy in the Toronto Fiero club that has had a 4.3 for some time.  It really hauls ass.  It has been reliable from what I know and he did it himself.  I'll try to get some more info from him.  Deffinately can be done.  Watch out for oBD2 electronics, unless someone out there has already cracked it for the 4.3.  Being different is what it is all about.<P>Jay<BR>86 GT Northstar <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> <BR>||||reg||1
